    "In 1912, during the Italo-Turkish War, Italy took Rhodes and the rest of the Dodecanese from the Ottomans. It held them until 1943, when it capitulated and the Germans took its place. The Italians left behind public buildings, roads and restored monuments, but also a policy, especially in the 1930s, that pushed the islanders to give up their language and schooling. Because the islands were Italian in 1923, their Muslim inhabitants were not part of the Greek-Turkish population exchange. By the Paris Peace Treaty of 1947 the Dodecanese went to Greece, and formal incorporation took place on 7 March 1948, still a public holiday on the islands.",
